#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166507 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f9dcbb0-d943-5340-916e-d789b6ab03bc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Value to What a Fourier Series Converges"}] :question/id #uuid "7e478876-8258-54a8-9dd7-81978b7cf9af" :question/math-academy-id "e-21865" :question/problem "${-1,\\begin{vmatrix}x ∈ [- \\frac{π}{2}, 0] \\\\ 2,\\end{vmatrix}x ∈ (0, \\frac{π}{2})$\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x) = \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} a_n\\cos\\bigg(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\bigg)+b_n\\sin\\bigg(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\bigg)\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x$, then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n$S(x) = f(x)$\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x$, then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n$S(x) = \\dfrac{f^-(x)+f^+(x)}{2}$\nwhere $f^-(x)$ and $f^-(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nThe one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{-}(\\frac{π}{2}) &= lim_(x → (π/2)^{-})f(x) = 2 \\\\\nf^{+}(\\frac{π}{2}) &= lim_(x → (π/2)^{+})f(x) =-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{f^-\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)+f^+\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)}{2} = \\dfrac{2+(-1)}{2} = \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\dfrac{1}{2}}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166508 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b3cb7656-1ce6-5b6a-9e88-5ba24a943c3f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of an Infinite Sum Using Fourier Series"}] :question/id #uuid "0e966ca9-eab2-5443-9581-9b7cb8ecd4b2" :question/math-academy-id "e-21866" :question/problem "${-1,\\begin{vmatrix}x ∈ [- π, 0] \\\\ 1,\\end{vmatrix}x ∈ (0, π)$\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n$f(x) \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{4}{\\pi(2n-1)} \\sin((2n-1)x)$.\nUse this Fourier series to find the sum of the series\n$\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that at\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left((2n-1)\\cdot\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = \\sin\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}+n\\pi\\right) = (-1)^{n+1}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\ninto the given Fourier series gives\n\n$$\nf\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{4}{\\pi(2n-1)} \\sin\\left((2n-1)\\cdot\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{4}{\\pi(2n-1)}(-1)^{n+1}\n$$\n\nNext, since $f(x)$ is continuous at\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nthe Fourier series converges to\n\n$$\nf\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nSince $[MATH: \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\in [0,\\pi),]$ we have\n\n$$\nf\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(4)/(π(2n - 1))(-1)^{n + 1} \\\\\n\\frac{π}{4} &= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)((-1)^{n + 1})/(2n - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} = \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167232 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b3cb7656-1ce6-5b6a-9e88-5ba24a943c3f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of an Infinite Sum Using Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d99ba2c0-4601-5438-bb70-f86447e6b5ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164949} {:answer/id #uuid "3c05ad62-4dc1-5c99-b84b-63a5c1adb3c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(− 1)^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164950} {:answer/id #uuid "5272d891-305b-5a29-83bd-9573ff4a6fdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(− 1)^{n + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164951} {:answer/id #uuid "5bf036e1-af29-5c0b-8e7d-16be34b78432" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164952}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5179c02c-5533-590b-b3e8-62fbb64458f5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164948}] :question/id #uuid "fb0887d0-dec5-559c-9512-f43e4278a2c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-342571" :question/problem "$F(x) = {0,\\begin{vmatrix}x ∈ [- π, 0] \\\\ 1,\\end{vmatrix}x ∈ (0, π)$\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $2π$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n$f(x)∼\\frac{1}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(2)/(π(2n - 1))\\sin ((2n - 1)x)$.\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.At $x = \\frac{π}{2}$, we have\n$(-1)^{n + 1}$ {{field-1}}"}]}
